WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.337 --> 00:00:03.021
- Hello, everyone, so
welcome to another episode

00:00:03.021 --> 00:00:04.838
of Unlocking Lily.

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:07.004
It's always been my dream to be a speaker

00:00:07.004 --> 00:00:10.911
and with the help of Evan,
I'm able to do this full-time

00:00:10.911 --> 00:00:14.893
and you guys are all coming
along for the ride with me.

00:00:14.893 --> 00:00:17.618
(air whooshing)
(impact echoing)

00:00:17.618 --> 00:00:21.124
So, Evan challenged me
to research 20 speakers

00:00:21.124 --> 00:00:24.506
and the reason for that is,
I'm starting out on my journey

00:00:24.506 --> 00:00:28.142
and the truth is, I can figure
everything out on my own,

00:00:28.142 --> 00:00:31.120
but it will take me a
very, very long time.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:33.120
So, to shorten that process,
it's important for me

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.062
to take a look at all the people

00:00:35.062 --> 00:00:38.333
who have done similar
things to what I want to do

00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:39.735
and I could look at their path,

00:00:39.735 --> 00:00:42.101
so, that way, I can could
fast-track my process

00:00:42.101 --> 00:00:43.797
and then model after them.

00:00:43.797 --> 00:00:45.494
So, I needed 20 speakers.

00:00:45.494 --> 00:00:48.369
I only had three, three people
that I've been following

00:00:48.369 --> 00:00:51.235
for the past few years,
but I needed 17 more.

00:00:51.235 --> 00:00:52.851
How do I do that, right?

00:00:52.851 --> 00:00:53.809
And so, I thought to myself,

00:00:53.809 --> 00:00:54.892
"Well, you know what?

00:00:54.892 --> 00:00:57.448
"Evan actually covers a
lot of successful people

00:00:57.448 --> 00:01:00.312
"on his channel, I'm sure
some of them are speakers,"

00:01:00.312 --> 00:01:01.145
and it's true.

00:01:01.145 --> 00:01:03.970
I did find a lot of them on his channel,

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:06.065
but I needed more than that

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:08.803
and, Google being our best friend,

00:01:08.803 --> 00:01:11.636
I actually Googled famous speakers

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:16.035
and I was able to come up
with my list of 20 names.

00:01:16.035 --> 00:01:18.004
And, once I got the 20 names,

00:01:18.004 --> 00:01:20.637
I split the research into two parts,

00:01:20.637 --> 00:01:23.828
so I had one part which
was a path to success

00:01:23.828 --> 00:01:26.866
and the other part is their business side.

00:01:26.866 --> 00:01:29.519
So, the path to success is very crucial.

00:01:29.519 --> 00:01:32.982
The reason for that is,
right now, I'm just starting,

00:01:32.982 --> 00:01:37.298
so for me to compare my
beginning to their top,

00:01:37.298 --> 00:01:39.210
is what they're doing right now, seems

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:41.222
like it's way too much of a leap

00:01:41.222 --> 00:01:44.870
and it almost seems impossible, at times.

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:47.825
But, if I were to actually
look at all the things

00:01:47.825 --> 00:01:50.333
that they have done in
their previous years

00:01:50.333 --> 00:01:52.161
and when they first got started, it may,

00:01:52.161 --> 00:01:54.652
not only does it make it
more realistic for me,

00:01:54.652 --> 00:01:56.855
but it also makes more sense.

00:01:56.855 --> 00:01:59.902
Now, okay, these are the
things that I need to do

00:01:59.902 --> 00:02:02.739
to get to where they are today.

00:02:02.739 --> 00:02:05.277
And, on the business
side, I want to be able

00:02:05.277 --> 00:02:07.999
to make an income from speaking,

00:02:07.999 --> 00:02:09.605
so for me to look at all the things

00:02:09.605 --> 00:02:13.558
that they are doing to earn an
income, it's going to help me

00:02:13.558 --> 00:02:16.408
create a business around
speaking, as well.

00:02:16.408 --> 00:02:17.646
So, as part of my research,

00:02:17.646 --> 00:02:20.063
I needed to highlight their breakthroughs.

00:02:20.063 --> 00:02:23.529
So, what are the specific
things that they did

00:02:23.529 --> 00:02:27.096
to break them through into
the successes they are today?

00:02:27.096 --> 00:02:29.738
And, as I was doing my
research, I started to realize

00:02:29.738 --> 00:02:31.981
that there were three common ways

00:02:31.981 --> 00:02:33.765
that they were breaking through.

00:02:33.765 --> 00:02:35.887
So, the first one is
(chimes ringing)

00:02:35.887 --> 00:02:38.493
to find a king maker,
so what that means is

00:02:38.493 --> 00:02:41.944
to leverage an existing brand or media.

00:02:41.944 --> 00:02:45.185
So, for example, Oprah
Winfrey and Les Brown were,

00:02:45.185 --> 00:02:46.609
had mediocre success,

00:02:46.609 --> 00:02:49.259
but they didn't actually
become household names

00:02:49.259 --> 00:02:51.574
until they had their own talk shows.

00:02:51.574 --> 00:02:54.846
And, even Jack Canfield,
who used to be an educator,

00:02:54.846 --> 00:02:57.995
and he didn't actually become
known for what he is today

00:02:57.995 --> 00:03:00.941
until he published his first book in his

00:03:00.941 --> 00:03:03.125
Chicken Soup for the Soul series.

00:03:03.125 --> 00:03:05.992
Number two is to grind out content.

00:03:05.992 --> 00:03:08.328
It's to work consistently on your craft,

00:03:08.328 --> 00:03:10.653
day after day after day.

00:03:10.653 --> 00:03:12.115
So, you have Neil Pasricha,

00:03:12.115 --> 00:03:16.282
who wrote on his blog, 1000
Awesome Things, every single day

00:03:17.235 --> 00:03:20.022
until, one day, he was recognized

00:03:20.022 --> 00:03:22.164
and he was able to get
a book deal out of it,

00:03:22.164 --> 00:03:23.780
which is The Book of Awesome,

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.119
and Eric Thomas, who
put out YouTube videos

00:03:26.119 --> 00:03:28.874
after YouTube videos after YouTube videos,

00:03:28.874 --> 00:03:33.038
until one of them went viral
and had over 40 million views.

00:03:33.038 --> 00:03:36.015
And, number three is to prove it live.

00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:38.550
These are the folks that went onstage

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:41.060
and actually proved
themselves and their message

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:42.778
and stood up for what they believed in.

00:03:42.778 --> 00:03:45.358
So, you have Tony Robbins
who was well-known,

00:03:45.358 --> 00:03:48.682
but did not become notorious
until he went onstage

00:03:48.682 --> 00:03:50.965
and cured a woman from her snake phobia

00:03:50.965 --> 00:03:54.228
after being challenged by a
psychiatrist that he was a fake,

00:03:54.228 --> 00:03:56.198
and even Brene Brown and Simon Sinek

00:03:56.198 --> 00:03:57.804
who were relatively unknown

00:03:57.804 --> 00:04:01.421
before they had their
world-popular TEDx talks,

00:04:01.421 --> 00:04:03.299
which actually put them on the map

00:04:03.299 --> 00:04:05.585
as being the leading thought leaders.

00:04:05.585 --> 00:04:08.003
So, on the business side,
I was able to identify

00:04:08.003 --> 00:04:10.754
three common ways they
were making an income,

00:04:10.754 --> 00:04:13.680
so number one, are
online training programs.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:16.647
So, Shawn Achor, Gabrielle
Bernstein and Zig Ziglar

00:04:16.647 --> 00:04:18.454
all have online courses

00:04:18.454 --> 00:04:20.668
that they sell through their website.

00:04:20.668 --> 00:04:22.317
Number two are books.

00:04:22.317 --> 00:04:24.714
Lisa Nichols, Robin
Sharma and Dale Carnegie

00:04:24.714 --> 00:04:28.380
all wrote best-sellers that
sold millions of copies.

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:31.580
Number three are live events and seminars.

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:34.435
Tony Robbins, Brendon
Burchard and Eckhart Tolle

00:04:34.435 --> 00:04:37.616
all sell out their shows months leading up

00:04:37.616 --> 00:04:39.418
to their live events.

00:04:39.418 --> 00:04:40.754
So, I walked into this research

00:04:40.754 --> 00:04:42.560
knowing I wanted to be a speaker,

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:45.284
but I'm walking away with
so much more clarity.

00:04:45.284 --> 00:04:46.797
On the breakthrough side,

00:04:46.797 --> 00:04:49.470
I love the people that proved it live.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:51.330
There's something so magical

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:54.459
and exhilarating to me about going onstage

00:04:54.459 --> 00:04:57.791
and showing yourself,
showing up as yourself

00:04:57.791 --> 00:04:59.843
and standing up for what you believe in

00:04:59.843 --> 00:05:02.636
and proving yourself and
proving your message.

00:05:02.636 --> 00:05:05.927
So, I really want to focus
my energy into doing that.

00:05:05.927 --> 00:05:08.426
And, on the business
side, I love coaching.

00:05:08.426 --> 00:05:11.764
I loved the one-on-one connection
that I get with people,

00:05:11.764 --> 00:05:14.524
not only that, to see them being inspired

00:05:14.524 --> 00:05:17.226
by their own awesomeness
and going out there

00:05:17.226 --> 00:05:19.969
and doing amazing things
and taking action.

00:05:19.969 --> 00:05:23.300
So, I'm going to focus a lot
of my energy into coaching

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:25.704
and, as much as I love
the one-on-one connection

00:05:25.704 --> 00:05:27.039
that I get from coaching,

00:05:27.039 --> 00:05:29.982
I love a group environment, as well.

00:05:29.982 --> 00:05:32.014
There's something so fulfilling to me

00:05:32.014 --> 00:05:34.484
when I see a group of
people support each other

00:05:34.484 --> 00:05:36.473
and have empathy for each other

00:05:36.473 --> 00:05:39.626
and then walk away as a
group feeling, you know,

00:05:39.626 --> 00:05:43.101
completely inspired and ready
to, like, take on their goals,

00:05:43.101 --> 00:05:44.825
it's such an amazing feeling.

00:05:44.825 --> 00:05:46.665
So, on the business side,
I'm going to really focus

00:05:46.665 --> 00:05:51.658
a lot of my time into the
coaching and the workshop side.

00:05:51.658 --> 00:05:54.424
So, this research gave me so much more

00:05:54.424 --> 00:05:56.780
than just a path to becoming a speaker.

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:59.048
It gave me confidence.

00:05:59.048 --> 00:06:01.291
A lot of the times, I think to myself

00:06:01.291 --> 00:06:03.790
that maybe my goal is too bold,

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:07.265
maybe I can't get there,
maybe it's a silly idea.

00:06:07.265 --> 00:06:08.433
You know, what makes me think

00:06:08.433 --> 00:06:10.893
that I could actually achieve
the same amount of success

00:06:10.893 --> 00:06:12.500
as all these people did?

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:14.146
You know, maybe I should have started

00:06:14.146 --> 00:06:15.162
when I was a lot younger.

00:06:15.162 --> 00:06:17.261
I don't have a lot of time.

00:06:17.261 --> 00:06:19.663
I need more education.

00:06:19.663 --> 00:06:21.106
I need to go back to school.

00:06:21.106 --> 00:06:22.601
I need to go get a PhD.

00:06:22.601 --> 00:06:23.806
Until that happens,

00:06:23.806 --> 00:06:26.908
I can't actually go
onstage and give a speech

00:06:26.908 --> 00:06:29.336
because I don't know
what I'm talking about,

00:06:29.336 --> 00:06:32.385
or I feel like I don't have the money

00:06:32.385 --> 00:06:35.941
to back up this business plan that I have.

00:06:35.941 --> 00:06:39.415
And, the truth is, a lot of
these people that I researched

00:06:39.415 --> 00:06:43.072
started off with a lot less
than what I have right now.

00:06:43.072 --> 00:06:44.921
I already have way more.

00:06:44.921 --> 00:06:47.043
You know, look at Dale Carnegie.

00:06:47.043 --> 00:06:48.660
He was actually homeless

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:50.699
when he started off his first seminar,

00:06:50.699 --> 00:06:52.917
he was living at the YMCA,

00:06:52.917 --> 00:06:55.954
and the things that he's accomplished,

00:06:55.954 --> 00:06:59.037
from having so little, is incredible.

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:02.650
Even years and years after his passing,

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:04.815
people know his training programs.

00:07:04.815 --> 00:07:06.034
They know his books.

00:07:06.034 --> 00:07:09.009
His books are still one of
the best-sellers in the world.

00:07:09.009 --> 00:07:12.009
So, what gives me the excuse?

00:07:12.009 --> 00:07:15.481
But, at the same time, it
makes me feel very confident.

00:07:15.481 --> 00:07:18.060
If he can do it, so can I

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:19.585
and that's really the truth, right?

00:07:19.585 --> 00:07:22.000
Anything that you want
to do is not too bold

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.868
because people who have done it before you

00:07:24.868 --> 00:07:26.696
have done it with a lot less.

00:07:26.696 --> 00:07:29.061
So, for my next challenge,
I'm going to take you guys

00:07:29.061 --> 00:07:32.515
on a deeper journey into the three people

00:07:32.515 --> 00:07:34.832
that I resonated with the most,

00:07:34.832 --> 00:07:38.906
so they are Tony Robbins,
Brene Brown and Simon Sinek.

00:07:38.906 --> 00:07:40.512
I'm going to share with you

00:07:40.512 --> 00:07:42.616
what are the specific things that they did

00:07:42.616 --> 00:07:44.740
to get to where they are today.

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:46.366
And, also, what are the things

00:07:46.366 --> 00:07:48.093
that I have learned from them

00:07:48.093 --> 00:07:50.917
that I can put into action right away?

00:07:50.917 --> 00:07:52.338
So, the question of the day is,

00:07:52.338 --> 00:07:55.527
who are the three people
that you look up to

00:07:55.527 --> 00:07:58.597
in your industry that you're
going to start researching today?

00:07:58.597 --> 00:08:00.345
I love reading your comments

00:08:00.345 --> 00:08:02.356
and I would love to find out who they are,

00:08:02.356 --> 00:08:04.173
so leave in the comments below

00:08:04.173 --> 00:08:06.268
and I'm going to join
in on the discussion.

00:08:06.268 --> 00:08:07.987
So, thank you so much for watching,

00:08:07.987 --> 00:08:12.138
but, more importantly, I am
so grateful for having you

00:08:12.138 --> 00:08:13.988
on this journey with me.

00:08:13.988 --> 00:08:16.297
You have been so
supportive and encouraging

00:08:16.297 --> 00:08:17.768
and loving since day one

00:08:17.768 --> 00:08:19.942
and, honestly, I couldn't have asked

00:08:19.942 --> 00:08:22.656
for a better community than Believe Nation

00:08:22.656 --> 00:08:25.751
to be part of this crazy ride that I'm on.

00:08:25.751 --> 00:08:28.647
And, I actually have a special
shout out for Karthik Reddy,

00:08:28.647 --> 00:08:29.950
so Karthik wrote,

00:08:29.950 --> 00:08:32.959
"Lily, you improved
Lily, congratulations."

00:08:32.959 --> 00:08:34.514
So, Karthik, thank you so much

00:08:34.514 --> 00:08:36.179
for writing the encouraging kind words.

00:08:36.179 --> 00:08:38.333
It really does mean a lot to me,

00:08:38.333 --> 00:08:40.604
so thank you and thank you guys

00:08:40.604 --> 00:08:42.139
and I'll see you very, very soon

00:08:42.139 --> 00:08:44.844
for another episode of Unlocking Lily.

00:08:44.844 --> 00:08:47.567
Thank you and I love you all (smooching).

00:08:47.567 --> 00:08:50.108
